B.ZB={[u.ck]:0,"Contraindicated in penicillin allergy.":1,"Avoid in suspected infectious mononucleosis (rash risk).":2,"Observation for 48-72 hours is appropriate only when immunocompetent and reassessment/treatment is feasible: age 6 months to 2 years, unilateral disease without otorrhea and non-severe symptoms; age 2 years or older, no otorrhea and non-severe symptoms.":3,"Severe symptoms: moderate or severe otalgia, otalgia lasting more than 48 hours, or temperature 39 C or higher. Use a 10-day course at any age when severe.":4,"Use the amoxicillin-clavulanate pathway if amoxicillin was given in the preceding 30 days, there is purulent conjunctivitis, recurrent AOM unresponsive to amoxicillin, or no improvement after 48-72 hours.":5,"TELL THE FAMILY HOW LONG IT WILL LAST, OR THEY WILL BUY AN ANTIBIOTIC - a cold itself runs about 7 to 10 days, though the cough can drag on for weeks afterwards. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961) In children it is reasonable simply to wait and watch for 2 to 4 weeks, since most of these coughs follow a virus and settle on their own. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":6,"A COUGH IS ONLY CHRONIC IN A CHILD AFTER FOUR WEEKS - by duration, a cough counts as acute under 3 weeks, subacute from 3 to 8 weeks, and chronic beyond 8 weeks - or beyond 4 weeks in a child aged 14 or under.":7,"REVIEW BEFORE ESCALATING - in children of 14 and under with no warning signs, the article's stepwise plan opens with spirometry and, where it can be done, a measurement of exhaled nitric oxide, and only then a chest X-ray. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) None of that belongs to a first presentation of an acute cough.":8,"NO HONEY DOSE IS PRINTED HERE. Both articles state honey as an option and neither states a quantity, a frequency or a duration, so no dose claim can be quoted from an opened document and none is invented. It is a spoonful given by a parent, not a prescription.":9,"SALINE, STEAM AND SUCTION ARE THE REST OF IT - a cool-mist humidifier, and sterile saline drops or spray, moisten the nose and help clear it. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":10,"NOTHING SHORTENS A POST-VIRAL COUGH - inhaled steroids, bronchodilators and oral medicines all fail to help a cough left behind by an infection. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":11,"KEEP THE SMOKE OUT OF THE HOUSE - press the family hard on keeping the child away from tobacco smoke. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":12,"AND MENTHOL RUBS ARE NOT FREE OF HARM - rubs made with menthol, camphor and eucalyptus may ease cough and blockage, but they can irritate the skin, the eyes and the nose. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":13,"IT DOES NOT TREAT THE COUGH. Paracetamol has no anti-inflammatory action. It is given for fever and discomfort, and a child who is comfortable without it does not need it.":14,"THE ROLE IS TO RECOGNISE, NOT TO PROVE - the healthcare team is not there to establish that maltreatment happened, nor to name who did it, but to pick out the children who may have been abused. The paediatric practitioner does not apportion blame or accuse a carer of a crime; the job is to say what injuries are there medically, to assess and treat the child, and to give families, investigators and lawyers medical information that is objective.":15,"CONSTIPATION IS THE COMMONEST FIXABLE CAUSE OF REPEAT INFECTIONS - screening for bowel and bladder dysfunction and treating it prevents UTI, is safe, works, and is thought of far too little. Asking about constipation, about wetting in the daytime, and about holding on will find the children who have it. The measures listed are voiding to a timetable every 3 to 4 hours, drinking enough, and treating the constipation itself. As for cranberry juice, the evidence to recommend it for preventing UTI is not there.":16,"WHEN THERE IS NOTHING WRONG, SAY SO PROPERLY - where the shortness is physiological, these children are medically well, so what needs managing is the effect on the child and the family. In constitutional delay, reassuring them is usually all that is required. In familial short stature, what matters is expectations that are realistic, and telling the family plainly that no disease underlies it.":17,[u.ou]:18,[u.k7]:19,[u.Hp]:20,"Look for an associated clavicle fracture, a sternoclavicular injury or a neurovascular insult on the same arm before settling for a sprain.":21,"The drug rows above are what the treating service gives. They are here so that the referral is an informed one and so the GP can recognise the regimen the patient comes back on - not as permission to start it without the referral.":22,"Never exceed 4 g in 24 hours in an adult, or 60 mg/kg in 24 hours in a child. MSF warns that paracetamol poisoning is severe - it causes hepatic cytolysis - and that the stated limits matter most in children and in older patients.":23,"MSF records no contra-indication in pregnancy or in breast-feeding, and names paracetamol as the analgesic of choice for a patient allergic to aspirin, one with a history of gastric trouble, a pregnant or breast-feeding woman, and children.":24,"NOT FOR SPUTUM COLOUR. On the article's own reading, purulent sputum need not mean bacterial infection and does not by itself call for an antibiotic - roughly 50% of those with acute bronchitis produce it.":25,[u.o2]:26,[u.uI]:27,"Contraindicated with MAO inhibitors or within 2 weeks of stopping them, in narrow-angle glaucoma, in acute coronary disease or cardiac asthma, in rhinitis sicca, and after transsphenoidal hypophysectomy.":28,"Use with care in coronary heart disease, hypertension, hyperthyroidism, diabetes, and prostatic enlargement causing difficulty passing urine.":29,[u.qu]:30,"The frequency here is conventional practice. The OTC label supports saline for dry and irritated nasal passages and says it is safe for frequent daily use, but prints no number of sprays and no schedule.":31,"Penicillin allergy is a contraindication - ask about prior reactions before prescribing.":32,"Blood-stained sputum needs separate urgent work-up rather than empirical antibiotics alone.":33,"A large daily volume of purulent sputum raises the possibility of bronchiectasis and should prompt referral rather than repeated antibiotic courses.":34,"Persistent symptoms with weight loss or night sweats need TB work-up before or alongside antibiotic treatment - a live consideration in Egypt.":35,"Avoid in penicillin allergy.":36,"Reduce dose in severe renal impairment.":37,"A rash occurs if amoxicillin is given in glandular fever.":38,"Red flag: features of a life-threatening deep space infection (diphtheria, peritonsillar/retropharyngeal/submandibular abscess) demand immediate intervention before routine antibiotic treatment.":39,"Red flag: confirmed peritonsillar abscess or other suppurative complication requires hospital-level management (drainage, IV antimicrobials), not outpatient oral antibiotics.":40,"NEVER UNDER ONE YEAR. The article puts the gate positively: honey eases how often and how badly a child coughs, may help adults too, and is an option from age 1 upwards. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) Below the first birthday honey must not be given, because of the risk of infant botulism. This is an absolute age gate, and honey is in every Egyptian kitchen.":41,"MIND THE OTHER BOTTLE IN THE HOUSE. Many Egyptian cold preparations already contain paracetamol; a parent giving a combination syrup plus a paracetamol syrup can pass the daily maximum without knowing.":42,"REDUCE IT IN THE MALNOURISHED CHILD - in severe acute malnutrition give 10 mg/kg, and no more than 3 doses in 24 hours.":43,"AND IN DENGUE WITH WARNING SIGNS - for a child, 10 mg/kg given 3 to 4 times a day.":44,"LIVER - use paracetamol with caution in hepatic impairment.":45,"ANTIBIOTICS DO NOT WORK HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MESSAGE - they do nothing for a common cold, yet they are handed out in something like 30% to 40% of these self-limiting infections, and that is a major driver of resistance. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961) The chest is no different: acute bronchitis is nearly always viral and is managed supportively, with neither antibiotics nor antivirals shown to help. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":46,"WHEN AN ANTIBIOTIC IS ACTUALLY INDICATED - group A beta-haemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis; severe acute otitis media; sinusitis running beyond 10 days; or sinus symptoms that worsen again after first improving. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961) Nothing else listed here is an antibiotic indication.":47,"NO COUGH SYRUP IS PRINTED HERE. Cough and cold preparations sold over the counter do not work in children under 12, and have killed children under 2 by overdose; decongestants and antihistamines are therefore not advised below 12. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) The common-cold article sets its own floor lower still: these medicines should not be given at all below the age of 4, for safety and for lack of any benefit. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":48,"WHY THEY ARE DANGEROUS, NOT MERELY USELESS - below the age of 6, both prescribed and over-the-counter cough and cold medicines carry real risk, and an overdose of an antihistamine or a decongestant can kill. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":49,"CODEINE - do not use it in children. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":50,"DEXTROMETHORPHAN - THE DOCUMENTS DISAGREE, AND BOTH ARE ON THE CARD. The clinical articles find no proven benefit in children for either dextromethorphan or codeine as cough suppressants, whether alone or combined with guaifenesin, and no definite benefit in adults either. The Egyptian formulary allows the syrup from 6 years and contraindicates it below that age; the tablets are contraindicated below 12 years altogether. It is not offered as a row here because no opened document shows a benefit in a child.":51,"BENZONATATE - its safety and effectiveness are not properly established, and it is not advised below the age of 10. (Upper Respiratory Tract Infections With Focus on The Common Cold, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK532961)":52,"RED FLAG - THE FEATURES THAT MEAN THIS IS NOT A CHEST COLD: abnormal observations, low oxygen, blue lips, a change in consciousness, vomiting, pneumonia that keeps coming back, a weakened immune system, trouble swallowing, and a child who has gone off food. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":53,"RED FLAG - INHALED FOREIGN BODY: a story of choking, in a child above all, should put inhalation of a foreign body on the list. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) A cough that started in one second, in a well child, is a foreign body until a chest is imaged.":54,"RED FLAG - PNEUMONIA: where pneumonia is suspected the usual work-up is a chest X-ray and a full blood count, with CRP checked selectively - a CRP over 30 mg/L in a fitting clinical picture makes pneumonia more likely. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) Otherwise these are clinical diagnoses needing no imaging or blood tests unless something warns of a complication - that covers acute bronchitis, most viral infections of the upper airway, acute rhinosinusitis and allergic rhinitis. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":55,"UNDER TWO WITH WHEEZE AND FAST BREATHING IS BRONCHIOLITIS, NOT THIS - bronchiolitis mostly strikes below age 2. It opens with upper-airway symptoms, then wheeze, crackles, fast breathing, indrawing, and sometimes fever. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221)":56,"A WET COUGH THAT WILL NOT STOP IS A DIFFERENT DIAGNOSIS - protracted bacterial bronchitis is a wet cough going on 4 weeks or more, with normal spirometry and a chest film that is normal apart from possible peribronchial cuffing, and it clears on 2 weeks of the right antibiotic. (Cough: Evaluation and Management, StatPearls/NCBI Bookshelf, NBK493221) That two-week course is a decision made after four weeks of cough, not at the first visit.":57,"SAFE WHERE ASPIRIN IS NOT - paracetamol is the preferred choice for anyone allergic to aspirin, for those with a history of stomach trouble, in pregnancy and breastfeeding, and in children.":58,"RED FLAG - Some clinical findings (high fever, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, otitis, cervical adenopathy, wheezing, productive cough, or dyspnea) add complexity and warrant further investigation for complications or an alternative diagnosis.":59,"RED FLAG - Antibiotics ARE indicated for severe acute otitis media, sinusitis lasting more than 10 days, or sinus symptoms worsening after initial improvement - they are not routinely needed otherwise.":60,"Never on its own. NICE NG245 1.6.3 is a flat prohibition: do not prescribe a short-acting beta 2 agonist, at any age, to a person with asthma unless an ICS is prescribed with it. Reliever-only treatment is what kills asthmatics.":61,"Budesonide is not for acute relief of bronchospasm.":62,"TIME-CRITICAL EMERGENCY: Give IM adrenaline IMMEDIATELY upon recognizing anaphylaxis (airway, breathing, or circulation compromise).":63,"IV adrenaline only in specialist settings and by clinicians skilled and experienced in its use. Seek expert help early for persistent respiratory or cardiovascular problems after 2 IM doses.":64,"Position patient flat with legs elevated (or sitting up if breathlessness dominates); DO NOT allow patient to stand or walk abruptly.":65,"Call for help early (resuscitation team or ambulance) and arrange emergency assessment.":66,"There is a signal of harm, not just absence of benefit: early steroid use is associated with a higher rate of intensive care admission even after adjusting for how sick the patient was. Very low-certainty evidence, but it is the reason the routine recommendation was withdrawn.":67,"Never prioritise this over adrenaline, fluids, or an adrenaline infusion. If reaching for hydrocortisone delays any of those, do not reach for it.":68,"Corticosteroids must not be given preferentially to adrenaline.":69,"Use risk-stratified in-hospital observation after anaphylaxis; a senior clinician should decide further treatment and observation duration for each patient.":70,"14-day duration yields higher eradication than 7 days.":71,"The PPI runs with either regimen. If you drop to the line 2 triple therapy, omeprazole 20 mg twice daily continues alongside the clarithromycin and amoxicillin - it is not left behind on line 1.":72,"CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy and breastfeeding - the tetracycline component binds fetal bone and teeth; defer eradication or refer.":73,"First-line because it avoids clarithromycin altogether. Guidelines set the cut-off for empirical clarithromycin triple therapy at 15% local resistance; Egypt\u2019s actual rate is not documented in the cited sources.":74,"Avoid alcohol due to metronidazole; take tetracycline with full glass of water.":75,"Cost: branded bismuth quadruple (BISPYLLER, TRIGASTCARE) runs 650-800 EGP. Cheaper Egyptian options exist (clarithromycin- or levofloxacin-based combinations), but local resistance makes them non-preferred first-line choices.":76,"Same drug as the omeprazole in regimen A. You give it with whichever regimen you choose, not with both - the regimens are alternatives.":77,"Use only where clarithromycin susceptibility is confirmed, or where local resistance is known to be below 15%. Egypt\u2019s clarithromycin resistance rate is not documented in the cited sources.":78,"All three are given together for the full course. Clarithromycin or amoxicillin on its own does not eradicate H. pylori - it fails and it selects for resistance.":79,"After each treatment, decontaminate patients\u2019 and close contacts\u2019 clothes and bed linen: wash at 60\xb0C or higher and dry in the sun, expose to sunlight, or seal in a plastic bag for 72 hours.":80,"Type I and II injuries do not necessarily need specialist review; provide the conservative care above and reassess recovery.":81,"Type III to VI injury needs orthopaedic or sports-medicine assessment; type IV to VI injuries typically need surgical management.":82,"RED FLAG - Evaluate for associated clavicle fracture, sternoclavicular injury, or neurovascular insult on the affected extremity.":83,"Antimicrobial therapy is not routinely indicated unless bacterial etiology is suspected.":84,"For children over 5 years with suspected atypical bacterial etiology, use the azithromycin pathway instead of amoxicillin.":85,"Infants under 3 months require initial inpatient therapy. Inpatient or complicated pneumonia has different therapy and duration; this is an outpatient reference.":86}
