B.azJ=new A.h("hypertensive-retinopathy","Amlodipine","Hypertension, age \u22656 years: initially 2.5 mg orally daily; titrate to response (max 10 mg/day). Investigate secondary hypertension.","oral.solid","Egyptian National Drug Formulary - Cardiovascular Chapter 2024 (amlodipine monograph, Hypertension, chronic). That blood pressure control is the treatment for this condition, and the classes used for it, come from Hypertensive Retinopathy - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf (NBK525980) - https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525980/, Treatment / Management","Blood pressure - the treatment for the retinopathy","2.5 to 5 mg once daily initially; evaluate the response after 2 to 4 weeks and titrate as needed to a maximum of 10 mg once daily. If more control is needed, consider combination therapy","long-term",u.aU)
B.awC=new A.h("hypothyroidism","Levothyroxine","Acquired hypothyroidism: pediatric endocrinology; initially 12.5\u201350 micrograms/day, adjust every 2\u20134 weeks using thyroid tests. Not congenital/newborn dosing.","oral.solid",u.vv,null,u.sC,"long-term",u.Qi)
B.aAa=new A.h("immunodeficiency","Immunoglobulin","Primary humoral immunodeficiency, age \u22652 years: immunology-prescribed infusion; source replacement regimen in Notes.","injection",'GAMUNEX-C (immune globulin injection, human, 10% liquid) US prescribing information, DailyMed set id ade6b84a-e95b-0a49-3296-f56208fdf35b, section 2.1 Recommended Dosage - verbatim: "The dose of GAMUNEX-C for patients with PI is 300 mg/kg to 600 mg/kg body weight (3 mL/kg to 6 mL/kg) administered every 3 to 4 weeks." The indication comes from Immunodeficiency Disorders (Primary and Secondary) - StatPearls (NBK500027) - verbatim: "Immunoglobulin replacement therapy is the mainstay of treatment for antibody deficiencies (X-linked agammaglobulinemia, common variable immunodeficiency, hyper-IgM syndromes, and other conditions with impaired antibody production)."',"Antibody replacement (specialist-initiated)","300 mg/kg to 600 mg/kg by intravenous infusion, every 3 to 4 weeks","Lifelong in an antibody deficiency, unless a transplant cures it",u.Bw)
B.aAQ=new A.h("infant-feeding-difficulty","Vitamin D","Birth\u20131 year: vitamin D 8.5\u201310 micrograms (340\u2013400 IU) orally daily if breastfed or formula intake <500 mL/day; prevention only.","oral.liquid","Breastfeeding - StatPearls (NCBI Bookshelf NBK534767) - https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534767/","If breastfed or taking less than 500 mL formula daily: give vitamin D; prevention, not feeding treatment",null,null,u.gE)
B.axS=new A.h("intermenstrual-pain","Paracetamol",u.u,"oral.solid",u.m,null,u.U,"As needed for pain or fever",u.o)
B.aDR=new A.h("intertrigo","Flucloxacillin",u.bY,"oral.solid",'Intertrigo - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf (NBK531489) - disease-level clinical article (intertrigo-full.txt) for the indication - verbatim: "Flucloxacillin or erythromycin, which targets gram-positive bacteria such as Staphylococcus or Streptococcus, should be prescribed if oral medication is necessary." Dose from Egyptian National Drug Formulary - Antimicrobial 2023, flucloxacillin monograph - verbatim: "The usual adult dose orally or by intramuscular injection is 250 mg four times daily" and "All systemic doses may be doubled in severe infections."',"Bacterial superinfection - oral","250 mg orally four times daily; the dose may be doubled for a severe infection","The formulary states no course length for this dose - review at 5 to 7 days rather than repeating a prescription",u.rf)
B.ayw=new A.h("irritable-bowel-syndrome","Loperamide","No pediatric IBS-diarrhoea indication; adult indication starts at 18. Acute-diarrhoea dosing must not be carried across.","oral.solid","Egyptian National Drug Formulary (endf-git.txt)",null,"Initial dose: 4 mg, followed by 2 mg after each loose stool.","As needed for diarrhea flares",u.VW)
B.aCg=new A.h("irritable-infant","Assessment, caregiver support and safety-netting (no routine medicine)","Unwell infant, sudden persistent crying, poor feeding/growth or safety concern: escalate. Typical well/thriving infant: reassurance, settling support and early follow-up; no routine medicine.",null,"No dose - AS13 assessment/support pathway; no routine medicine follows from the label alone",null,"Not applicable (infant presentation)","Assessment and early follow-up; escalation depends on the sourced red flags or suspected cause",u.Ww)
B.awx=new A.h("kawasaki-disease","Immunoglobulin","Hospital treatment: IV immunoglobulin 2 g/kg once over 10\u201312 h, with aspirin; urgent admission.","injection","Kawasaki Disease - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537163/","Immunoglobulin","Not applicable - Kawasaki disease is a disease of childhood (see the paediatric dose)","A single infusion",u._S)
B.aCJ=new A.h("kwashiorkor","Vitamin A",u.sb,"oral.solid",u.pm,"Micronutrient replacement",u.GQ,"Throughout the treatment period",u.ww)
B.aDo=new A.h("liver-cirrhosis","Ursodeoxycholic acid","No established pediatric primary-biliary-cholangitis regimen; cystic-fibrosis dosing is a different indication.","oral.solid","Egyptian National Drug Formulary - Gastrointestinal Tract 2025 (ursodeoxycholic acid monograph)","Underlying liver disease","Primary biliary cholangitis: 12-16 mg/kg/day. Give in 3 divided doses for the first 3 months, then as a single dose in the evening","Indefinite - the formulary states treatment in primary biliary cholangitis may be continued indefinitely",u.FT)
B.ayW=new A.h("malaria-uncomplicated","Primaquine","Fever-hospital specialist: review G6PD and pregnancy/lactation status before radical cure; no routine child regimen displayed.","oral.solid","Primaquine phosphate tablets, film coated (sanofi-aventis U.S. LLC) US prescribing information, Indications and Usage, Contraindications, Warnings and Dosage and Administration (DailyMed SetID 1bfbf4ae-81b8-4160-a00d-6322aadd4b59)","If confirmed P. vivax or P. ovale and G6PD result plus pregnancy/lactation status are reviewed; add to selected blood-stage treatment","Given alongside chloroquine, not instead of it. The US label dose is 1 tablet (26.3 mg, equivalent to 15 mg base) once daily for 14 days, to clear liver-stage parasites; it may be taken with or without food.",'Fourteen days, and the course is what prevents the relapse rather than what treats the attack. US label verbatim: "daily for 14 days", and the drug is "recommended only for the radical cure of vivax malaria, the prevention of relapse in vivax malaria, or following the termination of chloroquine phosphate suppressive therapy in an area where vivax malaria is endemic."',u.RG)
B.ax6=new A.h("measles","Immunoglobulin","Measles exposure: urgent product-specific IM immunoglobulin assessment within 6 days; do not give with measles vaccine. Vaccine timing changes.","injection","GAMASTAN (immune globulin (human)) injection US prescribing information, section 1 Indications and Usage and section 2 Dosage and Administration (DailyMed SetID 561f48de-2697-4a35-8233-bd1cdb73438f)","Post-exposure prophylaxis for a contact who cannot have the vaccine - intramuscular only",'0.25 mL/kg intramuscularly. US label verbatim, from its dosing table: "0.25 mL/kg" with the instruction "Administer to a susceptible person within 6 days of exposure." For an immunocompromised contact the label doubles it: "0.5 mL/kg Immediately administer (maximum dose, 15 mL) to an immunocompromised child." Route is not a choice - "For intramuscular use only." and "Do not administer intravenously." Large volumes are split: "Divide and inject doses greater than 10 mL into several muscle sites to reduce local pain and discomfort."','One dose, tied to the exposure rather than to symptoms. The label\'s window is six days from contact, and an immunocompromised child is dosed "Immediately".',u.kl)
B.auu=new A.h("melaena","Esomeprazole","Melaena: emergency pediatric referral; do not use adult bolus/infusion or unrelated antisecretory schedule.","injection",u.Gm,u.Qg,u.hn,u.X,u.BB)
B.ayO=new A.h("meningitis-suspected","Ceftriaxone","Urgent transfer; no source-authored single pre-transfer dose. Below 1 month, cefotaxime preferred; receiving pediatric team doses meningitis.","injection",u.vK,null,u.kP,u.of,u.za)
B.avb=new A.h("meningitis-suspected","Chloramphenicol","Monitored IV regimen: neonates 25 mg/kg/day in 4 doses; older children 50 mg/kg/day, or 50\u2013100 mg/kg/day if severe, in 4 doses (max 4,000 mg/day). Plasma target 15\u201325 micrograms/mL; >25 toxic.","injection","Egyptian National Drug Formulary - Antimicrobial 2023 (chloramphenicol monograph, Serious infections including bacterial meningitis)",null,"IV: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 hours, maximum 4 g daily, with plasma concentration monitoring. The formulary gives no single pre-hospital dose","A hospital regimen; there is no sourced single dose to give before transfer",u.sW)
